// Public main (2026-08-30) made gunbc run REFUSE when no cgroup memory limit binds
// the process and GUNBC_MEMORY_BUDGET_BYTES is unset (gunbc.host_budget_source):
// GitHub-hosted runners bind no memory.high/memory.max, so every roster invocation
// panicked before resolving. This declares the slot bound the refusal asks for -
// 6 GiB, inside the hosted runner's 7 GiB - scoped to the one step that runs gunbc.
fn run_witnesses_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Run the private witness roster over the composed root" },
    id: none,
    run: witness_run_script(),
    shell: none,
    env: Present { value: [kv(key: "GUNBC_MEMORY_BUDGET_BYTES", value: yaml_int(n: 6442450944))] },
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// THE SECTION 4c WALL THIS REPOSITORY DID NOT HAVE.
//
// gunbc run executes a witness without the source-annotation attachment check; gunbc compile is
// the only instrument that performs it, and until this step nothing here invoked it. Measured
// 2026-09-12 on private main: nineteen blocking "source annotation sits inside a declaration body"
// refusals in one file, which arrived through six review rounds with both lanes green. Two claims,
// kept apart. CI green was correct and uninformative about this class: no executed invocation here
// performed the check, because every recorded one was gunbc run. That does NOT vindicate the reviews:
// a reader could have caught these by eye, or by invoking gunbc compile, and none did. What the
// evidence establishes is that nothing DID run the check, not that nothing COULD.
//
// A STEP, NOT A JOB. The job roster is closed to growth because a job is a standing claim on a
// paid runner on every push and pull request. This step runs on the runner the witnesses job
// already claims, over the binary it already built and the composed root the roster step already
// assembled -- it therefore runs after the roster, on !cancelled() so a red roster does not hide a
// section 4c red, and refuses rather than greens when that composed root is absent.
//
// THE SUBJECT IS THE ENFORCED ROSTER'S CLOSURE, NOT THE WHOLE PRIVATE ROOT, and the narrowing is
// forced rather than chosen. A whole-root compile is refused at arm time by
// gunbc.whole_corpus_compile_admission, whose declared demand was measured on a dag-primary public
// compile and is above the budget this job declares; that refusal fires on the private-primary
// root too, which is far smaller than the subject the demand was measured on. Lying to the
// admission with a larger declared budget would be the fail-open DESIGN section 5 forbids. So the
// step compiles --entry over roster_aggregate, with the roster's own source_roots_argv, which is
// admitted and reaches every module an enforced witness imports.
//
// WHAT THAT LEAVES UNCHECKED, stated rather than implied: private modules no enforced witness
// imports -- the known-red witnesses and their unique imports, the must-not-compile probes, the
// projection drift witness, and modules nothing imports at all. A section 4c violation there still
// passes this step. The restoration trigger names the capability and is carried as the typed row
// annotation_closure_narrowing_dissolution, which the step echoes on every run.
//
// THE BUDGET IS NOT THE ONLY BARRIER, and the trigger says so. Because the step passes only on exit
// 0, widening to the whole root would pull the known-red witnesses and must-not-compile probes into
// the gated compile, and their expected refusals would make it exit 1 however cheap it became. So the
// trigger names both halves -- affordable admission AND separating the expected-refusal population
// from the gated compile -- and an affordable admission alone must not be read as sufficient. An
// earlier wording named affordability alone, and that was never sufficient for the guarantee: under
// the earlier gate a whole-root compile could be afforded AND the step could pass before annotation
// checking ran, so affordability never established a working section 4c check over every private
// module. The exit-0 pass condition did not create that gap, it exposed it: the trigger had been
// consistent with a defective gate, which is why nothing contradicted it.
// THE PASS CONDITION IS A COMPILE THAT EXITS 0 AND CARRIES NO SECTION 4c LINE. Unrelated blocking
// errors are NOT tolerated: any of them makes the compile exit 1, which fails this step. The family
// prefix decides only the WORDING of that failure -- a located section 4c FAIL when a line starting
// "source annotation " is present, otherwise a refusal as not established. That prefix is shared by
// the trailing, body and unattached forms. Exit 0 is a precondition the subject can meet: the
// known-red witnesses and must-not-compile probes, whose refusals are expected, sit outside the
// enforced roster closure this step compiles.
//
// A LOG THAT WAS NEVER WRITTEN IS NOT CLEAN. Exit 0 is a clean compile; exit 1 must carry the
// renderer's terminal error-count line; any other exit (137 is an OOM kill) refuses before the grep,
// so an empty match over an unwritten log can never read as zero violations.
// A REFUSING COMPILE WITH NO SECTION 4c LINE IS NOT CLEAN EITHER. Exit 1 can come from a phase that
// halts before annotation attachment runs, so the absence of a match there is not evidence. Only exit
// 0 passes; exit 1 fails when it carries section 4c lines and otherwise refuses as not established.
// The enforced roster closure compiles with zero blocking errors, so this costs no false red today.
//
// THE READER GETS THE SAME TREATMENT AS THE COMPILER. grep's no-match status 1 is the only
// status that means zero violations; any other status is a read failure and refuses. No sort sits
// in that path, because a pipeline absorbed with || true once let a failing sorter print PASS over a
// log carrying a located refusal. The compiler exit and the reader status are both captured inside an
// if, and only the display grep of the error-count line ends in || true, because the runner's default
// shell is bash -eo pipefail, which would otherwise end the step before these checks classify it.
data annotation_refusal_prefix: NonEmptyStr = "source annotation "

// THE SHELL IS CARRIED AS A STRING, the medium every run script in this module already uses, and it
// is transport rather than authority: the step's decisions are declared here, but their realization is
// hand-written bash. Its dissolution is a typed row, echoed by the step, beside the closure narrowing's.
data annotation_step_shell_medium_dissolution: NonEmptyStr = "gunbc emits orchestration to a shell target from a modeled step, so the section 4c step's exit-status and reader classification become typed arms instead of hand-written bash carried as string fragments"

data annotation_closure_narrowing_dissolution: NonEmptyStr = "a section 4c check over every private module can both be afforded and pass: gunbc compile admits the private primary population within the witnesses job's declared budget, priced on the subject actually compiled rather than on the public corpus's demand, AND the expected-refusal population (known-red witnesses, must-not-compile probes) is separable from the compile this step gates on, so their expected refusals do not defeat the exit-0 pass condition. Affordable admission alone is not sufficient: it does not establish that the expanded population can exit 0. Only when both hold, drop --entry and compile every private module"

fn annotation_compile_script() -> String {
  join([
    "set -u\n",
    "GUNBC=\"$PWD/", public_checkout_path, "/target/release/gunbc\"\n",
    "COMPOSE=\"", compose_root_env_default, "\"\n",
    "OUT=\"$\{RUNNER_TEMP:-/tmp}/gunbc-private-annotation-compile\"\n",
    "if [ ! -d ", private_overlay_dir, "/dag ]; then\n",
    "  echo \"::error::REFUSE section 4c check observed nothing: the composed root $COMPOSE is absent, so the roster step did not reach composition.\"\n",
    "  exit 1\n",
    "fi\n",
    "cd \"$COMPOSE\"\n",
    "echo \"section 4c subject is the enforced roster closure, not every private module. Dissolves when: ", annotation_closure_narrowing_dissolution, "\"\n",
    "echo \"this step is hand-written shell carried as a string. Dissolves when: ", annotation_step_shell_medium_dissolution, "\"\n",
    "rm -rf \"$OUT\" \"$OUT.log\"\n",
    "unset GUNBC_RESOLVED_GRAPH_CACHE_DIR || true\n",
    "unset GUNBC_NATIVE_CACHE_ROOT || true\n",
    "if \"$GUNBC\" compile ", source_roots_argv, " --entry ", private_entry_prefix, roster_aggregate.entry, " --output-dir \"$OUT\" --target dag > \"$OUT.log\" 2>&1; then code=0; else code=$?; fi\n",
    "echo \"gunbc compile exited $code\"\n",
    "if [ \"$code\" != \"0\" ] && [ \"$code\" != \"1\" ]; then\n",
    "  tail -n 40 \"$OUT.log\"\n",
    "  echo \"::error::REFUSE section 4c check observed nothing: gunbc compile exited $code, which is neither a clean compile nor a rendered refusal.\"\n",
    "  exit 1\n",
    "fi\n",
    "if [ \"$code\" = \"1\" ] && ! grep -qE 'error\\(s\\)' \"$OUT.log\"; then\n",
    "  tail -n 40 \"$OUT.log\"\n",
    "  echo \"::error::REFUSE section 4c check observed nothing: gunbc compile exited 1 without its terminal error-count line.\"\n",
    "  exit 1\n",
    "fi\n",
    "grep -E 'error\\(s\\)' \"$OUT.log\" | tail -n 1 || true\n",
    "if grep -F -- \"", annotation_refusal_prefix, "\" \"$OUT.log\" > \"$OUT.annotations\"; then\n",
    "  cat \"$OUT.annotations\"\n",
    "  echo \"::error::FAIL section 4c: source annotations outside module-item grain, each located above. Move each above the module-scope declaration it describes.\"\n",
    "  exit 1\n",
    "else\n",
    "  reader=$?\n",
    "  if [ \"$reader\" != \"1\" ]; then\n",
    "    echo \"::error::REFUSE section 4c check observed nothing: the diagnostic reader exited $reader, which is a read failure and not the no-match status 1.\"\n",
    "    exit 1\n",
    "  fi\n",
    "fi\n",
    "if [ \"$code\" != \"0\" ]; then\n",
    "  tail -n 40 \"$OUT.log\"\n",
    "  echo \"::error::REFUSE section 4c check not established: gunbc compile refused on diagnostics outside the section 4c family, so it may have stopped before annotation attachment ran. Fix those diagnostics; only a compile that exits 0 can pass this step.\"\n",
    "  exit 1\n",
    "fi\n",
    "echo \"PASS  section 4c: no source-annotation refusal over the closure of ", roster_aggregate.entry, "\"\n",
  ], "")
}

fn annotation_compile_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Check source annotations (DESIGN section 4c) over the composed root" },
    id: none,
    run: annotation_compile_script(),
    shell: none,
    env: Present { value: [kv(key: "GUNBC_MEMORY_BUDGET_BYTES", value: yaml_int(n: 6442450944))] },
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: "!cancelled()" },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// SELF-HOSTED (operator 2026-08-30): the gunb-ai hosted-minutes pool is exhausted and
// the business IS self-hosted CI minutes - the private roster dogfoods the same
// [self-hosted, linux, arm64] fleet the public floor already runs on.
// Constructed from the extdeps label vocabulary (gunbc#9743), not string literals:
// the three default labels every self-hosted runner carries, derived from the typed
// kernel and architecture; custom labels join through the third argument when the
// fleet starts assigning them.
fn witnesses_job() -> Job {
  Job {
    id: "witnesses",
    name: none,
    runner: SelfHosted { labels: self_hosted_labels(kernel: Linux, arch: Aarch64, custom: []) },
    steps: [
      checkout_private_step(),
      checkout_public_step(),
      record_public_revision_step(),
      setup_rust_step(),
      build_gunbc_step(),
      run_witnesses_step(),
      annotation_compile_step(),
    ],
    needs: [],
    env: none,
    outputs: none,
    if_condition: none,
    timeout_minutes: 90,
    continue_on_error: none,
    concurrency: none,
    permissions: none
  }
}

// THE FOUR-HOURLY TIMER IS AN OPERATOR-APPROVED INTERIM (A SCAFFOLD), NOT THE INSTRUMENT WE WANT.
//
// APPROVAL: operator ruling 2026-09-14. DESIGN section 5 requires scaffold approval to be external
// to the diff, because an author who can write a scaffold can equally write a row claiming it was
// approved; this row records the ruling, it does not constitute it.
//
// WHAT IT BUYS. This repository composes against PUBLIC main resolved at run time (public_root_ref
// above), and until now the only triggers were push, pull_request and manual dispatch -- all three
// driven by PRIVATE activity. Nothing re-resolved the composition when PUBLIC main moved. So between
// a public merge and the next private push, private main did not go red: it went SILENT, holding a
// green that predated the cause. Receipt: private main last ran 2026-09-14T06:52:02Z green; public
// gunbc#11310 merged at 07:02:38Z deleting AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjection and three siblings from
// gunbc.roadmap_authority; private main did not run again for hours, reporting green the whole time
// over a composition that no longer existed. A stale green is silent wrongness (DESIGN section 5);
// a red that names no cause is merely expensive. This timer converts the first into the second.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT BUY, STATED PLAINLY BECAUSE IT IS THE COST: A SCHEDULED RED NAMES NO CAUSE. It
// fires on the clock, not on the public merge, so it cannot say which public change invalidated the
// composition, it lands on no author, and it arrives up to four hours after the fact. It is an
// approximation of the trigger this actually wants, and it is chosen only because that trigger does
// not exist yet.
//
// DISSOLUTION TRIGGER -- THE CAPABILITY, NOT AN ARTIFACT (DESIGN section 4b(3)): repository_dispatch
// modelled END TO END in extdeps.github.actions WorkflowTrigger -- the constructor, its arm in
// workflow_trigger_is_merge_group, and its arm in the serializer match in
// extdeps.languages.yaml.gha_workflow -- AND public merge to main firing it at gunbc-private. That
// whole capability is what makes a red name its cause. NOT satisfied by the constructor existing,
// by this row being revisited, or by the timer being tuned. When it lands, DELETE this row and the
// Schedule trigger together; the timer is not kept as a belt-and-braces second path, because a
// second trigger for one question is the parallel authority DESIGN section 3 forbids.
//
// WHY 4 HOURS AND NOT HOURLY. The interval IS the worst-case silent window. Below the run's own
// duration (about 22 minutes, measured) a shorter interval buys nothing because runs queue on one
// runner; and private pushes already close the window for free whenever they happen, so the timer
// only has to cover the gaps, which are overnight-shaped. Hourly costs four times as much for a
// window push traffic mostly closes anyway. Renders as "0 0,4,8,12,16,20 * * *".
data witnesses_interim_schedule: CronSchedule = CronSchedule {
  minute: Exact { value: 0 },
  hour: Listed { values: [0, 4, 8, 12, 16, 20] },
  day_of_month: Wildcard,
  month: Wildcard,
  day_of_week: Wildcard,
}

// THE SELF-HOSTED POOL, AND THE CANCELLATION RISK THAT CHOICE CARRIES.
//
// THE HEADING THIS BLOCK USED TO CARRY WAS WRONG AND IS CORRECTED HERE. It read "A GITHUB-HOSTED
// RUNNER, DELIBERATELY, AND NOT THE FLEET'S SELF-HOSTED POOL" while witnesses_job directly below
// declared, and still declares, SelfHosted { labels: self_hosted_labels(kernel: Linux,
// arch: Aarch64) } -- and while the annotation forty lines above says the roster "dogfoods the same
// [self-hosted, linux, arm64] fleet the public floor already runs on". The emitted YAML agrees with
// the declaration: runs-on: [self-hosted, linux, arm64]. So the heading asserted the opposite of the
// row it sat on. The contradiction PREDATES e0bfda2 (2026-08-30, runner labels constructed from the
// extdeps vocabulary): before that commit the field already read SelfHosted, so that refactor did
// not reverse a decision, and this was a stale claim rather than a silent flip.
//
// THE MEASUREMENT UNDERNEATH IT IS EVIDENCE AND IS KEPT. Measured 2026-08-26: the self-hosted pool
// cancelled 39 percent of 200 runs inside three and a half hours and destroyed 943 minutes of wall
// clock in long cancellations. It was cited FOR the proposition that adding this repository's load
// to that pool would make the private signal as unreliable as the thing it is supposed to check.
// That argument is still the right argument; only the claim about which runner this job uses was
// false. This job runs on the pool that measurement is about.
//
// RE-MEASURED 2026-09-14, because the schedule below adds load to exactly that pool and the
// 2026-08-26 reading predicted what that would cost. Two readings, carried rather than averaged:
//   - THIS workflow: 200 runs, 2026-09-13T03:20Z to 2026-09-14T15:34Z -- 152 success, 45 failure,
//     3 still running, ZERO cancelled.
//   - The PUBLIC floor on the SAME pool: 300 runs over the preceding 10 hours -- 12.7 percent
//     cancelled.
// So the pool has NOT become clean and the 2026-08-26 measurement is NOT retired. Cancellation is
// concentrated in the public floor's long, high-churn jobs and was not observed for this job at all.
// The honest limit: the two populations differ in arrival rate, and a 36-hour window is not the
// three-and-a-half-hour burst the original measured. This establishes nothing about a burst.
//
// WHAT IT DOES ESTABLISH is the only thing the schedule needs. This workflow already runs about 133
// times a day on push and pull_request. A four-hourly timer adds six: a 4.5 percent increase in this
// job's load on the pool. The 2026-08-26 argument was about ADDING THIS REPOSITORY'S LOAD to the
// pool; that load is already there, and the timer is a rounding error on it.
data private_witness_workflow: Workflow = Workflow {
  name: "witnesses",
  run_name: none,
  on: [
    WorkflowDispatch { inputs: [] },
    Push { branches: ["main"], paths: [] },
    PullRequest {
      branches: ["main"],
      types: [Opened, Synchronize, Reopened, ReadyForReview]
    },
    Schedule { cron: witnesses_interim_schedule },
  ],
  concurrency: none,
  jobs: [witnesses_job()],
  env: none,
  permissions: Present { value: WorkflowPermissions {
    contents: Present { value: PermRead },
    pull_requests: none,
    issues: none,
    actions: none,
    id_token: none
  } }
}

fn expected_witnesses_yml() -> String {
  serialize_yaml(v: project_workflow_to_yaml(workflow: private_witness_workflow))
}

// THE VERIFIER MUST NOT BE DISCOVERED BY THE ARTIFACT IT VERIFIES, and the first shape of this
// repair was exactly that mistake. Enrolling the drift witness in private_witnesses puts its run
// step inside witnesses.yml -- so the precise failure the witness exists to catch, a committed
// witnesses.yml left at bytes that omit the new step, deletes the verifier along with the step.
// GitHub executes the stale YAML, the old roster passes, and the job greens over the drift. That is
// the inert-lens defect one level up: the check is present in the authority and absent from what
// actually runs.
//
// So the drift check is emitted to its OWN workflow file, whose existence GitHub discovers
// independently of anything expected_witnesses_yml() produces. Removing the drift step from
// witnesses.yml no longer removes the check, because the check was never in witnesses.yml.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLOSE, stated rather than implied: this workflow verifies itself as well as
// witnesses.yml, but a committed projection-drift.yml that dropped its own run step would not be
// caught by anything here -- nothing outside the pair is watching the pair. That residue is why the
// rung is mechanically preventable and not structural: safety still depends on this file executing.
// Closing it needs a required status check bound by name outside the repository's own YAML, which
// is a branch-protection fact this repository cannot declare.
fn projection_drift_run_script() -> String {
  join([
    "set -u\n",
    "cd ", public_checkout_path, "\n",
    "GUNBC=\"$PWD/target/release/gunbc\"\n",
    "PRIVATE_ROOT=\"$(cd .. && pwd)\"\n",
    "COMPOSE=\"", compose_root_env_default, "\"\n",
    "rm -rf \"$COMPOSE\"\n",
    "git worktree prune\n",
    "git worktree add --detach \"$COMPOSE\" HEAD\n",
    "mkdir -p ", private_overlay_dir, "\n",
    "cp -a \"$PRIVATE_ROOT/dag\" ", private_overlay_dir, "/dag\n",
    "ln -sfn \"$PRIVATE_ROOT\" \"$COMPOSE/private_repo\"\n",
    "cd \"$COMPOSE\"\n",
    "echo \"private_overlay is a generated ingest copy of the private checkout, composed into a detached worktree OUTSIDE the repository root so no auto-push can reach it; do not edit it. Edit the private checkout. Dissolves when: ", overlay_copy_dissolution, "\"\n",
    "echo \"LOCAL RUNS: ", overlay_local_method, "\"\n",
    "unset GUNBC_RESOLVED_GRAPH_CACHE_DIR || true\n",
    "unset GUNBC_NATIVE_CACHE_ROOT || true\n",
    "\"$GUNBC\" run ", source_roots_argv,
    " --entry \"", private_entry_prefix, projection_drift_witness.entry,
    "\" --function \"", projection_drift_witness.function, "\"\n",
  ], "")
}

fn run_projection_drift_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Check every committed projection against its authority" },
    id: none,
    run: projection_drift_run_script(),
    shell: none,
    env: Present { value: [kv(key: "GUNBC_MEMORY_BUDGET_BYTES", value: yaml_int(n: 6442450944))] },
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

fn projection_drift_job() -> Job {
  Job {
    id: "projection-drift",
    name: none,
    runner: SelfHosted { labels: self_hosted_labels(kernel: Linux, arch: Aarch64, custom: []) },
    steps: [
      checkout_private_step(),
      checkout_public_step(),
      record_public_revision_step(),
      setup_rust_step(),
      build_gunbc_step(),
      run_projection_drift_step(),
    ],
    needs: [],
    env: none,
    outputs: none,
    if_condition: none,
    timeout_minutes: 60,
    continue_on_error: none,
    concurrency: none,
    permissions: none
  }
}

data projection_drift_workflow: Workflow = Workflow {
  name: "projection-drift",
  run_name: none,
  on: [
    WorkflowDispatch { inputs: [] },
    Push { branches: ["main"], paths: [] },
    PullRequest {
      branches: ["main"],
      types: [Opened, Synchronize, Reopened, ReadyForReview]
    },
  ],
  concurrency: none,
  jobs: [projection_drift_job()],
  env: none,
  permissions: Present { value: WorkflowPermissions {
    contents: Present { value: PermRead },
    pull_requests: none,
    issues: none,
    actions: none,
    id_token: none
  } }
}

fn expected_projection_drift_yml() -> String {
  serialize_yaml(v: project_workflow_to_yaml(workflow: projection_drift_workflow))
}

// THE PROJECTION HAD NO CONSUMER, WHICH IS THE INERT-LENS TIER RATHER THAN COVERAGE. This module
// declares itself the authority for the workflow files below, and the expected_* functions compute
// the bytes that authority implies -- but until this row nothing called them. A committed file and
// its declared producer could disagree indefinitely and every witness would still pass, because no
// witness asked. Machinery that exists while nothing gates on it is worse than absent: the
// annotation at the top of this module claims the YAML is emitted, and that claim was checked by a
// human reading the diff, which is exactly the manual step a projection exists to remove.
//
// THE DESTINATIONS ARE NOW MODELLED TOO. The paths lived only inside that annotation, so the one
// fact tying each producer to the file it produces was unreachable from the graph -- a projection
// that cannot say where it lands cannot be compared against what landed.
data witnesses_yml_path: NonEmptyStr = ".github/workflows/witnesses.yml"

data projection_drift_yml_path: NonEmptyStr = ".github/workflows/projection-drift.yml"

// The two files above are emitted by THIS module, so this is their authority. It is a row and
// not a literal at each use because the drift refusal, the enrolled rows and the controls all
// cite it, and three spellings of one module name is the fork this repository keeps paying for.
data private_witness_workflow_authority: NonEmptyStr = "strategy.private_witness_workflow"

// THE EXPECTATION CROSSES AS DATA -- A PATH AND ITS BYTES -- NOT AS A GENERATOR. Each row carries
// what the authority says the file should contain, already computed, so the observer folds over
// rows rather than dispatching to a producer. No function value is carried, which is the shape
// public #9808 established when it dissolved fn-valued transforms into declared rows, and it is the
// shape a shared public/private drift observer would need if one is ever built.
type ProjectedArtifact {
  path: NonEmptyStr
  expected: String
  authority: NonEmptyStr
}

fn projected_artifacts() -> List<ProjectedArtifact> {
  [
    ProjectedArtifact {
      path: witnesses_yml_path,
      expected: expected_witnesses_yml(),
      authority: private_witness_workflow_authority,
    },
    ProjectedArtifact {
      path: projection_drift_yml_path,
      expected: expected_projection_drift_yml(),
      authority: private_witness_workflow_authority,
    },
  ]
}

// The comparison takes BOTH sides as parameters rather than reading the disk or calling the
// producer itself. That keeps it a pure decision the witness can put a discriminating RED through
// without perturbing the working tree: drift is authorable as an argument here, where a
// disk-reading predicate would make the refusal expressible only by corrupting a committed file.
type ProjectionAgreement
  = ProjectionAgrees
  | ProjectionDrifted { detail: String }

// THE REFUSAL NAMES THE PRODUCER YOU HAVE TO RE-RUN, and it reads that name off the row rather
// than from a literal here. The literal said strategy.private_witness_workflow for every artifact,
// which was true while this module emitted both enrolled files and became false the moment the
// node-rental page, the two buyer CSVs and the plan tracker were enrolled -- four of six rows named
// a module that does not produce them, so the diagnostic sent a fixer to regenerate the wrong
// thing. A drift refusal whose remedy sentence is wrong costs more than no sentence.
fn projection_agreement(
  path: NonEmptyStr,
  authority: NonEmptyStr,
  committed: String,
  expected: String,
) -> ProjectionAgreement {
  if committed == expected {
    ProjectionAgrees
  } else {
    ProjectionDrifted {
      detail: join([
        "committed ", path,
        " disagrees with its authority ", authority,
      ], "")
    }
  }
}

// A READ REFUSAL IS NOT DRIFT AND KEEPS ITS OWN ARM. An unreadable file and a file whose bytes
// disagree have opposite remedies -- repair the composition versus regenerate the projection -- and
// the filesystem substrate already hands us that distinction as FilesystemReadSucceeded |
// FilesystemReadRefused. Collapsing both into "drifted" would erase it at the very next fold, which
// is the state-space conflation the substrate spent a type preventing. The fold takes the read
// OUTCOME as a parameter, so a synthetic refusal fixture can assert the refusing arm exactly,
// rather than the arm being reachable only through a real filesystem failure.
type ProjectedArtifactObservation
  = ProjectedArtifactObserved { path: NonEmptyStr, agreement: ProjectionAgreement }
  | ProjectedArtifactReadRefused { path: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }

fn projected_artifact_observation(
  path: NonEmptyStr,
  authority: NonEmptyStr,
  read: FilesystemReadOutcome,
  expected: String,
) -> ProjectedArtifactObservation {
  match read {
    FilesystemReadSucceeded { content } =>
      ProjectedArtifactObserved {
        path: path,
        agreement: projection_agreement(
          path: path, authority: authority, committed: content, expected: expected),
      }
    FilesystemReadRefused { error } =>
      ProjectedArtifactReadRefused { path: path, cause: error }
  }
}

fn projected_artifact_observation_ok(o: ProjectedArtifactObservation) -> Bool {
  match o {
    ProjectedArtifactObserved { path, agreement } => match agreement {
      ProjectionAgrees => true
      ProjectionDrifted { detail } => false
    }
    ProjectedArtifactReadRefused { path, cause } => false
  }
}

fn projected_artifact_observation_detail(o: ProjectedArtifactObservation) -> String {
  match o {
    ProjectedArtifactObserved { path, agreement } => match agreement {
      ProjectionAgrees => join(["", path, " agrees with its authority"], "")
      ProjectionDrifted { detail } => detail
    }
    ProjectedArtifactReadRefused { path, cause } =>
      join(["could not read committed ", path, ": ", cause], "")
  }
}
